<p class="gna-lede">The most common signs of obstructive sleep apnea in adults are loud, regular snoring, pauses in breathing or gasping that someone else notices, and feeling unrefreshed or sleepy during the day despite a full night in bed. None of these alone proves you have it — only a sleep study can confirm the diagnosis — but together they are a strong reason to get checked, because untreated sleep apnea is linked to high blood pressure, heart rhythm problems and drowsy driving.</p>

<h2 id="what-is-sleep-apnea">What is obstructive sleep apnea?</h2>
<p>Obstructive sleep apnea (OSA, also spelt apnoea) happens when the soft tissues of the throat — the soft palate, tonsils and tongue — relax during sleep and narrow or block the airway. Breathing becomes shallow (a hypopnea) or stops altogether (an apnea) for ten seconds or more. Oxygen levels dip, the brain briefly rouses you to reopen the airway, and the cycle repeats, sometimes dozens of times an hour. Most people have no memory of these awakenings; they simply wake up tired.</p>
<p>Doctors measure severity with the <strong>apnea–hypopnea index (AHI)</strong>, the average number of breathing events per hour of sleep. In adults, 5–14 events an hour is usually called mild, 15–29 moderate, and 30 or more severe. Severity, together with your symptoms and general health, shapes which treatments make sense.</p>
<div class="gna-keypoints">
  <h3>Key points</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Loud snoring with witnessed pauses or gasping, plus daytime sleepiness, is the classic pattern — but women and slimmer adults often have quieter, less typical symptoms.</li>
    <li>Questionnaires such as STOP-BANG estimate your risk; they cannot diagnose or rule out sleep apnea.</li>
    <li>A sleep study — in a sleep laboratory or, for selected adults, at home — is the only way to confirm it and measure severity.</li>
    <li>Untreated moderate to severe sleep apnea is associated with high blood pressure, atrial fibrillation, stroke and road accidents.</li>
    <li>Effective treatments exist, and most of them do not involve surgery.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<h2 id="night-time-symptoms">Night-time symptoms: what your partner may notice</h2>
<p>Because the breathing events happen while you are asleep, the first person to notice is often a spouse, parent or roommate. The night-time signs include:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Loud, habitual snoring</strong> most nights, not just after a heavy dinner or during a cold. It is often irregular — loud snores broken by silence.</li>
  <li><strong>Witnessed pauses in breathing</strong>, followed by a snort, choke or gasp as breathing restarts.</li>
  <li><strong>Waking with a choking sensation</strong> or a pounding heart.</li>
  <li><strong>Restless, broken sleep</strong> — tossing and turning and frequent awakenings.</li>
  <li><strong>Getting up to pass urine</strong> two or more times a night (nocturia), which sleep apnea can provoke even when the bladder and prostate are healthy.</li>
  <li><strong>Night sweats</strong>, and a <strong>dry mouth or sore throat</strong> on waking from breathing through the mouth.</li>
</ul>
<p>Snoring on its own is very common and does not always mean sleep apnea. Our guide to <a href="/blogs/snoring-vs-sleep-apnea-difference/">snoring vs sleep apnea</a> explains how to tell the two apart.</p>

<h2 id="daytime-symptoms">Daytime symptoms adults often overlook</h2>
<p>The daytime effects build slowly, so many people put them down to work pressure, long commutes or simply getting older:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Feeling unrefreshed after seven or eight hours in bed</li>
  <li>Excessive sleepiness — dozing while reading, watching television, in meetings or as a passenger</li>
  <li>Morning headaches that ease within an hour or two of getting up</li>
  <li>Poor concentration, forgetfulness and slower reactions</li>
  <li>Irritability, low mood or anxiety</li>
  <li>Reduced interest in sex, or erectile difficulties</li>
  <li>Relying on tea, coffee or energy drinks to get through the afternoon</li>
</ul>
<h3>Symptoms can look different in women</h3>
<p>Women are more likely to describe fatigue, insomnia, morning headaches or low mood than loud snoring and gasping, and their snoring is often quieter. The risk rises noticeably after menopause and can increase during pregnancy. As a result, sleep apnea in women is frequently missed or put down to something else.</p>
<h3>Body weight is only part of the picture</h3>
<p>Excess weight, especially around the neck and abdomen, is a major risk factor — but slimmer adults develop sleep apnea too. A small or set-back lower jaw, a narrow upper jaw, enlarged tonsils, a long soft palate and long-standing nasal blockage can all crowd the airway; our article on <a href="/blogs/can-jaw-surgery-help-with-sleep-apnea/">how jaw position affects sleep apnea</a> explains why. Research suggests South Asian adults tend to develop sleep apnea at a lower body weight than people of European descent, so a "normal" BMI should not be reassuring on its own. Evening alcohol and sleeping tablets, smoking, being male, being over 50 and a family history of sleep apnea also raise the risk.</p>

<h2 id="stop-bang-screening">STOP-BANG: a quick way to estimate your risk</h2>
<p>STOP-BANG is a short, widely used screening questionnaire, first developed to identify patients at risk before surgery. Score one point for each "yes":</p>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Letter</th><th>Question</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><strong>S</strong> — Snoring</td><td>Do you snore loudly — loud enough to be heard through a closed door, or for your partner to nudge you?</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>T</strong> — Tired</td><td>Do you often feel tired, fatigued or sleepy during the day?</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>O</strong> — Observed</td><td>Has anyone seen you stop breathing, choke or gasp during sleep?</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>P</strong> — Pressure</td><td>Do you have, or are you being treated for, high blood pressure?</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>B</strong> — BMI</td><td>Is your body mass index more than 35?</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>A</strong> — Age</td><td>Are you older than 50?</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>N</strong> — Neck</td><td>Is your neck circumference more than 40 cm, measured around the Adam's apple?</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>G</strong> — Gender</td><td>Are you male?</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>Broadly, a score of 0–2 suggests a low risk, 3–4 an intermediate risk and 5–8 a high risk of moderate to severe sleep apnea. Two cautions apply. First, a low score does not rule the condition out, particularly in women, younger adults and people with a lower BMI, where the questionnaire is less sensitive; researchers have tested lower BMI and neck cut-offs for Asian populations for exactly this reason. Second, a high score is not a diagnosis — it means you should have a proper assessment. Doctors often pair it with the <strong>Epworth Sleepiness Scale</strong>, which asks how likely you are to doze in eight everyday situations.</p>
<div class="gna-callout gna-callout--warn">
  <p><strong>Drowsy at the wheel?</strong> If you have nodded off or drifted out of your lane while driving, or had a near miss because of sleepiness, stop driving and get assessed promptly. People with untreated sleep apnea have a higher risk of road crashes. If you are involved in an accident or someone is seriously unwell, call 108 or go to the nearest emergency department.</p>
</div>

