<h2 id="sleep-study">How sleep apnea is diagnosed: the sleep study</h2>
<p>A diagnosis needs an objective record of your breathing during sleep. There are two main options, and a sleep physician or pulmonologist will advise which suits you.</p>
<h3>In-laboratory polysomnography</h3>
<p>You spend a night in a sleep laboratory, usually in a hospital. Small adhesive sensors on the scalp, face, chest, legs and finger record brain waves, eye and leg movements, airflow, breathing effort, heart rhythm and blood oxygen. This is the most complete test, and it is preferred when the picture is unclear, when you have heart or lung disease or a neuromuscular condition, or when another sleep disorder is suspected.</p>
<h3>Home sleep apnea testing</h3>
<p>A simpler portable device records breathing, airflow and oxygen while you sleep in your own bed. It suits many otherwise healthy adults with a high likelihood of moderate to severe sleep apnea. Because it does not measure sleep itself, it can underestimate severity, so a negative or borderline home test in someone with clear symptoms is usually followed by a laboratory study.</p>
<p>Your report will show the AHI, the lowest oxygen level reached and whether events are worse when you lie on your back. A maxillofacial surgeon adds a different piece of the puzzle: examining the jaw relationship, bite, palate, tongue and nose and, where relevant, reviewing scans to see where the airway is narrowest.</p>

<h2 id="health-risks">Why it matters: heart, blood pressure and other risks</h2>
<p>Each breathing pause causes a fall in oxygen and a surge of stress hormones that push up heart rate and blood pressure. Repeated night after night, this strain is linked with:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>High blood pressure</strong>, including blood pressure that stays high despite three or more medicines</li>
  <li><strong>Atrial fibrillation</strong> and other heart rhythm problems</li>
  <li><strong>Heart failure, coronary artery disease and stroke</strong></li>
  <li><strong>Type 2 diabetes</strong> and poorer blood-sugar control</li>
  <li><strong>Depression</strong>, memory problems and a lower quality of life</li>
  <li><strong>Road traffic and workplace accidents</strong> caused by sleepiness</li>
</ul>
<p>It is worth being precise about what the evidence shows. These conditions are clearly <em>associated</em> with sleep apnea, and treatment reliably improves sleepiness, concentration and quality of life and can modestly lower blood pressure. Whether treatment prevents heart attacks and strokes is still being studied, and results so far are mixed. That uncertainty is a reason to manage your heart and blood-pressure risks alongside sleep apnea — not a reason to ignore it.</p>
<p>For professional drivers — cab, lorry, bus and delivery drivers — and anyone who drives long distances, untreated sleepiness is a safety issue for other road users as well as for yourself.</p>

<h2 id="when-to-see-specialist">When to see a specialist</h2>
<p>Book an assessment with your doctor, a sleep physician or a maxillofacial surgeon if:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Someone has seen you stop breathing or gasp during sleep</li>
  <li>You snore loudly most nights and feel sleepy or unrefreshed during the day</li>
  <li>Your STOP-BANG score is 3 or more</li>
  <li>You snore and have hard-to-control blood pressure or atrial fibrillation, or have had a stroke</li>
  <li>You have dozed off while driving or at work</li>
  <li>You have a small or receding lower jaw, or a bite problem, along with snoring</li>
</ul>
<p>Children who snore most nights, breathe through the mouth or pause in their sleep should be seen by a paediatrician or ENT specialist; the causes and treatment in children are different.</p>

<h2 id="treatment-and-expectations">What treatment looks like — and what it can realistically achieve</h2>
<p>Treatment depends on severity, symptoms, anatomy and your own preferences. For moderate to severe sleep apnea, <strong>CPAP</strong> (continuous positive airway pressure) — a bedside machine that keeps the airway open with gentle air pressure through a mask — remains the first-line treatment. Other options include custom oral appliances that hold the lower jaw forward, positional therapy, weight management, treatment of nasal blockage and, for carefully selected patients, surgery such as <a href="/blogs/maxillomandibular-advancement-for-osa/">maxillomandibular advancement</a>. Our guide to <a href="/blogs/cpap-alternatives-for-sleep-apnea/">CPAP alternatives</a> compares them in detail.</p>
<p>Each option has trade-offs. CPAP works well when it is worn, but some people find the mask hard to get used to. Oral appliances can cause jaw or tooth discomfort and gradual bite changes. Surgery carries the risks of any operation, including swelling, numbness and a recovery period of several weeks, and results vary from person to person. Whatever you choose, a follow-up sleep study is usually advised to check that treatment is working.</p>
